    "product_id": "cf3af3f6-af39-4809-a12d-4644af61e5ce",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 250909\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n209 AM PDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Temperatures continue to warm through Saturday. \n\n* Increasing confidence in clouds, rainfall, and cooler readings \n  Sunday through Tuesday as tropical moisture spreads northward.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Thursday.\n\nA weak ridge will keep temperatures on the warm side with highs up \nto 5 degrees above-normal (mid 90s to low 100s in the desert \nvalleys) and lows around 5 to 10 degrees above-normal (the 70s). \nThis pattern breaks late-weekend leading to cooler conditions and \nbuilding cloud cover along with increasing humidity as a deep trough \napproaches the area and pulls moisture from the south.\n\nModel agreement is really improving on the trough digging into the \nregion late-weekend and centering over southern Nevada by Monday \nevening. This will lead to an initial moisture tap arriving into the \nwestern protons of the CWA into Sunday, offering spotty storm and \nshower activity focused over Arizona. As the trough continues to \nbuild through Sunday, tropical moisture from what will be the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo begin to interact with that potion of the \njet stream and pull northward. The main focus of the push of \nmoisture looks to be well to our east; however, the low pressure \ncenter associated with the trough will wrap some of the moisture \ninto southern Nevada. This will lead to strong chances for showers \nand storms across the eastern half of the CWA, with the best chances \nfocused over Mohave County. The sheer-rich environment along with \nthe cooler temperatures around the low pressure center could lead to \nbetter chances for severe storms Monday afternoon and into that \nnight. Rain chances look to exit Tuesday afternoon with quieter \nweather expected for the rest of the forecast period.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package... \nExpect light easterly winds late-morning. Winds will veer through \nthe afternoon, ultimately resulting in gusts to 20 kts from the \nsouthwest. Cannot rule out gusts from the southeast for a couple of \nhours as this directional transition occurs. There is a 10 percent \nchance of an occasional gust over 20 kts. Gusts fall off after \nsunset, with breezy southwesterly winds continuing overnight around \n8 kts. No operationally significant cloud cover through the TAF \nperiod.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Expect gusty \nsouthwesterly winds to 20 kts across the Las Vegas Valley and Owens \nValley this afternoon. Gusts will favor the west at KDAG, while the \nColorado River Valley TAF sites remain light and diurnal. No \noperationally significant cloud cover through the TAF period.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
